 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "SBAB6 Entrepreneurship: recognises the tools of the doctor, the cook, the painter and the gardener; like Elif, invents the job of their own dreams (“flower doctor”) and takes the first step towards it.",
   "TAKB.2 Building the content of the speaking process: connects the topic with prior knowledge and daily life by talking about days they went to the doctor and the tools at home and at school.",
   "E1.1 Curiosity: like Elif, asks “What's this? And what's this?”; explores the tools by touching and listening.",
   "SDB2.3 Social Awareness: notices how people doing different jobs in the community help us (doctor, teacher, cook, gardener, painter).",
   "D3 Diligence: sees from Dr Aslı's words “At school! I studied for years.” that learning a job takes effort; does the check-up in order and with care."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Show the cover: “Who could the person next to Elif be? How can you tell?” Listen to the guesses without correcting them.",
   "Show a closed bag: “What might be in a doctor's bag?” Draw what the children say on the board.",
   "Ask: “What job does someone you know do? What would you like to be when you grow up? It's fine if you don't know.” Accept every answer."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 1, point to Elif's thoughtful face: “Why is Elif thinking?” Say that it is perfectly natural not to know what you'll be when you grow up.",
   "On page 2, let the children touch each tool and repeat what Dr Aslı says: “It listens to your heart, it takes your temperature, it covers a scrape.”",
   "On page 3, touch Miss Nur: she says “Will you ask me before you listen?” Practise asking permission together, “May I listen?”, before listening to someone's heart; both predictions are good ideas.",
   "On page 4, Elif asks Fluffy for permission too. Say the words “first, then, last” together; let the children find their own hearts with their hands.",
   "On page 5, ask “Who works with this tool?”: painter, gardener, cook (the cook's wooden spoon). If anyone notices the droopy flowers on the shelf, keep it as a clue for the next page.",
   "On page 6, Elif finds the answer herself; listen to her choice without commenting, then ask the children about their dreams."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "What was in Dr Aslı's bag? What was each thing for?",
   "How did Dr Aslı learn her job? How do you learn something new?",
   "What does Elif want to be when she grows up? Why do you think so? Which job would you like to do?"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "The Jobs Basket and My Dream Card",
   "sure": "25 min",
   "materyal": "Safe real tools such as a toy doctor's kit, a paintbrush, a watering can, a wooden spoon and a book; a basket; paper and crayons",
   "adim": [
    "Put the tools in the basket. Each child draws one tool, explores it by touch and says “This is for ...”.",
    "As a class, talk about “Who uses this tool?”; discover that one tool can be used in more than one job.",
    "Each child draws the job they want to do when they grow up and one tool for that job; it can also be a brand-new job they have imagined (like a flower doctor).",
    "Hang the pictures on the “Dream Board”; the children take turns to talk about their own picture, and the others ask one question."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "What tools do we use at home? Who uses them, and what for?",
   "When you went to the doctor, which tools did the doctor use?",
   "What do you want to be when you grow up? What would you need to learn for that?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Jobs at Home, Tools at Home",
   "d": "Take a walk around the house together and find three tools used for a job (like a ladle, a broom or a clothes peg). Look at sharp tools together without touching them. Ask your child who uses each one and what for. Then let your child ask someone in the family or your neighbourhood who does a different job a question they have prepared: “How did you learn this job?”"
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “What Will I Be When I Grow Up?” On Jobs Day, Elif didn't know what she would be when she grew up. She explored Dr Aslı's tools with curiosity, checked the teddy bear step by step, and in the end found the answer herself: a flower doctor! At home, listening to your child's dreams without commenting and asking “What could we learn for that?” feeds their curiosity."
 },
